en  and it's quite right that across the  country people can make their  their appointment and come forward for  their vaccine  but in terms of numbers only a hundred  thousand were were boosted yesterday so  the numbers have really declined haven't  they  i think we you know we've just been  through two weekends of bank holiday and  i think that's you know quite clear that  numbers will change as we move forward  and what was really incredible is the  number of people coming forward on  christmas day and even on the bank  holidays we had more people coming  forward on the bank holidays than other  bank holidays in the past so people have  really taken the vaccine program to  heart and it's probably the most  successful vaccine program that the nhs  has ever run and probably one of the  most successful across the whole world  as well and i want to thank everybody  for playing their part whether it's the  nhs the thousands if millions of  volunteers that have really helped made  a difference and people coming forward  to get that that life-saving vaccine in  such a timely way  nevertheless we're seeing critical  incidents at a number of hospitals um  how how many exactly uh how many we  think about half a dozen nhs trusts have  had critical incidents because of staff  shortages how many exactly do you know  well i think the critical incidents are  announced and then there can be very  short term ones  and it's saying to the other trusts  around you can we have some extra help  can we have the mutual aid sometimes  it's just a matter of hours that the  critical instance in place for other  times it's longer but it's actually  reaching out to the the wider nhs to say  yeah we have got a problem in this  particular area and it is sometimes  quite geographical as well and for  different reasons it could be staff  shortages it could be other reasons so  it's quite wrong do you know how many  tests you know how many were out this  morning for example i know as you say it  